Title: The Book

Text:
Miriam came to the old house to recover a ledger. In the first room, the archivist's chair collapsed under him, but the shelf behind him held the books; he rose and leaned on the wall. In the hall, a clerk had rubbed a name from the register, and the owner seized his wrist, calling it robbery. A man stood in the doorway and announced himself master, yet the servants answered the woman with the seal. The map showed a passage through the east wall, but the floor ended at stone, so she trusted the stone. When the lamp failed in the corridor, she kept her hand on the plaster, and the wall led her to the door. In the yard, men lifted a sow onto a cart, and it leaped into the neighbor's pen, never into the sty it had left. Before opening the iron box, she wrote on a slate what finding would prove her guess false. The flood came and took the silver and the map, but the villagers remembered her promise, and that remained. She tried twelve keys in the lock; only the seventh turned it. When the bell rang once above the gate, she read it as one message and did not add a second meaning.

